ML_PAGE("l2-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 2 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Lecture 2 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l2\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"l2n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">88 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Chapter 2 — Machine Learning Pipeline, taught intuitively\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s0\" title=\"Chapter 2 — Machine Learning Pipeline, taught intuitively\">Chapter overview</button></li><li data-full=\"1. First understand the difference: CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s1\" title=\"1. First understand the difference: CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>CRISP-DM vs ML pipeline</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Business problems are not automatically ML problems\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s2\" title=\"2. Business problems are not automatically ML problems\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Business goal to ML task</button></li><li data-full=\"3. The ML Pipeline is narrower\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s3\" title=\"3. The ML Pipeline is narrower\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Project vs technical workflow</button></li><li data-full=\"4. The standard ML pipeline\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s4\" title=\"4. The standard ML pipeline\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Pipeline stages</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Why a pipeline matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s5\" title=\"5. Why a pipeline matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Why a pipeline matters</button></li><li data-full=\"6. The single most important rule in the chapter: preprocessing consistency\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s6\" title=\"6. The single most important rule in the chapter: preprocessing consistency\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Preprocessing consistency</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Why must preprocessing be identical?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s7\" title=\"7. Why must preprocessing be identical?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Reuse training parameters</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Supervised vs unsupervised pipelines\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s8\" title=\"8. Supervised vs unsupervised pipelines\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Supervised vs unsupervised pipelines</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Where does ML data actually come from?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s9\" title=\"9. Where does ML data actually come from?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Data sources</button></li><li data-full=\"10. CSV — already close to ML&#x27;s favourite shape\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s10\" title=\"10. CSV — already close to ML&#x27;s favourite shape\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>CSV</button></li><li data-full=\"11. JSON — hierarchical rather than flat\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s11\" title=\"11. JSON — hierarchical rather than flat\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>JSON</button></li><li data-full=\"12. XML\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s12\" title=\"12. XML\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>XML</button></li><li data-full=\"13. HTML and web scraping\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s13\" title=\"13. HTML and web scraping\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>HTML and web scraping</button></li><li data-full=\"14. SQL — where a lot of real business data lives\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s14\" title=\"14. SQL — where a lot of real business data lives\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>SQL databases</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Before cleaning anything: understand data types\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s15\" title=\"15. Before cleaning anything: understand data types\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Data types overview</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Numeric data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s16\" title=\"16. Numeric data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Numeric data</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Categorical data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s17\" title=\"17. Categorical data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Categorical data</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Datetime data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s18\" title=\"18. Datetime data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Datetime data</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Text data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s19\" title=\"19. Text data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Text data</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Data wrangling: the dirty work that actually matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s20\" title=\"20. Data wrangling: the dirty work that actually matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Data wrangling</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Example: what&#x27;s wrong with this dataset?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s21\" title=\"21. Example: what&#x27;s wrong with this dataset?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Spot the data problems</button></li><li data-full=\"22. First rule of cleaning: inspect before touching\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s22\" title=\"22. First rule of cleaning: inspect before touching\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Inspect before cleaning</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Why `info()` is useful\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s23\" title=\"23. Why info() is useful\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>info()</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Why `describe()` is useful\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s24\" title=\"24. Why describe() is useful\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>describe()</button></li><li data-full=\"25. Data quality checklist\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s25\" title=\"25. Data quality checklist\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Data quality checklist</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Clean column names\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s26\" title=\"26. Clean column names\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Renaming columns</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Selecting columns\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s27\" title=\"27. Selecting columns\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Selecting columns</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Filtering rows\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s28\" title=\"28. Filtering rows\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Filtering rows</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Data types can look correct to humans but be wrong to computers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s29\" title=\"29. Data types can look correct to humans but be wrong to computers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Fixing dates (to_datetime)</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Numeric strings\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s30\" title=\"30. Numeric strings\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Numbers stored as text</button></li><li data-full=\"31. Feature engineering — turning existing information into better information\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s31\" title=\"31. Feature engineering — turning existing information into better information\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Feature engineering</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Example: derive weekend\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s32\" title=\"32. Example: derive weekend\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Example: weekend flag</button></li><li data-full=\"33. `map()` — convert values using a mapping\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s33\" title=\"33. map() — convert values using a mapping\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>map() for value mapping</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Missing values\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s34\" title=\"34. Missing values\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Missing values</button></li><li data-full=\"35. Strategy 1: Remove\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s35\" title=\"35. Strategy 1: Remove\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Drop missing rows</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Strategy 2: Impute\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s36\" title=\"36. Strategy 2: Impute\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Impute missing values</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Mean vs median\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s37\" title=\"37. Mean vs median\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Mean vs median</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Strategy 3: Forward fill / backward fill\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s38\" title=\"38. Strategy 3: Forward fill / backward fill\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Forward / backward fill</button></li><li data-full=\"39. Important: missing-value handling is a reasoning problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s39\" title=\"39. Important: missing-value handling is a reasoning problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Ask why data is missing</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Duplicates\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s40\" title=\"40. Duplicates\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Duplicates</button></li><li data-full=\"41. Why duplicates are dangerous\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s41\" title=\"41. Why duplicates are dangerous\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>Why duplicates hurt</button></li><li data-full=\"42. Basic duplicate handling\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s42\" title=\"42. Basic duplicate handling\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>Handling duplicates</button></li><li data-full=\"43. Categorical data — computers don&#x27;t naturally understand `&quot;Red&quot;`\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s43\" title=\"43. Categorical data — computers don&#x27;t naturally understand &quot;Red&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Encoding categories</button></li><li data-full=\"44. Label encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s44\" title=\"44. Label encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>Label encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"45. When numerical coding makes sense\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s45\" title=\"45. When numerical coding makes sense\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>When numeric codes work</button></li><li data-full=\"46. One-Hot Encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s46\" title=\"46. One-Hot Encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>One-hot encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"47. Label encoding vs One-Hot Encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s47\" title=\"47. Label encoding vs One-Hot Encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>Label vs one-hot</button></li><li data-full=\"48. Feature scaling — why numbers can unfairly dominate\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s48\" title=\"48. Feature scaling — why numbers can unfairly dominate\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>Why scaling matters</button></li><li data-full=\"49. Scaling fixes the measuring-stick problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s49\" title=\"49. Scaling fixes the measuring-stick problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">49</span>Comparable units</button></li><li data-full=\"50. Min-Max Scaling\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s50\" title=\"50. Min-Max Scaling\"><span class=\"toc-no\">50</span>Min-max scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"51. Standardization / Z-score\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s51\" title=\"51. Standardization / Z-score\"><span class=\"toc-no\">51</span>Standardization (z-score)</button></li><li data-full=\"52. Scaling does NOT mean deleting information\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s52\" title=\"52. Scaling does NOT mean deleting information\"><span class=\"toc-no\">52</span>Scaling keeps the order</button></li><li data-full=\"53. Does every algorithm require scaling?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s53\" title=\"53. Does every algorithm require scaling?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">53</span>When scaling is needed</button></li><li data-full=\"54. Text preprocessing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s54\" title=\"54. Text preprocessing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">54</span>Text preprocessing</button></li><li data-full=\"55. Why text preprocessing exists\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s55\" title=\"55. Why text preprocessing exists\"><span class=\"toc-no\">55</span>Why clean text</button></li><li data-full=\"56. Data summarization: stop staring at 1,000 rows\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s56\" title=\"56. Data summarization: stop staring at 1,000 rows\"><span class=\"toc-no\">56</span>Summarizing data</button></li><li data-full=\"57. Understand `groupby()` using Split → Apply → Combine\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s57\" title=\"57. Understand groupby() using Split → Apply → Combine\"><span class=\"toc-no\">57</span>groupby: split–apply–combine</button></li><li data-full=\"58. Multiple aggregations\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s58\" title=\"58. Multiple aggregations\"><span class=\"toc-no\">58</span>Multiple aggregations</button></li><li data-full=\"59. Visualization — don&#x27;t model what you haven&#x27;t looked at\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s59\" title=\"59. Visualization — don&#x27;t model what you haven&#x27;t looked at\"><span class=\"toc-no\">59</span>Why visualize</button></li><li data-full=\"60. Chart selection should follow the question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s60\" title=\"60. Chart selection should follow the question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">60</span>Choosing a chart</button></li><li data-full=\"61. Line chart = how something changes\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s61\" title=\"61. Line chart = how something changes\"><span class=\"toc-no\">61</span>Line chart</button></li><li data-full=\"62. Bar chart = compare categories\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s62\" title=\"62. Bar chart = compare categories\"><span class=\"toc-no\">62</span>Bar chart</button></li><li data-full=\"63. Histogram = what does one numerical variable look like?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s63\" title=\"63. Histogram = what does one numerical variable look like?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">63</span>Histogram</button></li><li data-full=\"64. Box plot = spread + unusual values\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s64\" title=\"64. Box plot = spread + unusual values\"><span class=\"toc-no\">64</span>Box plot</button></li><li data-full=\"65. Scatter plot = relationship between two numeric variables\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s65\" title=\"65. Scatter plot = relationship between two numeric variables\"><span class=\"toc-no\">65</span>Scatter plot</button></li><li data-full=\"66. Now the concept that ruins a shocking number of ML experiments: data leakage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s66\" title=\"66. Now the concept that ruins a shocking number of ML experiments: data leakage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">66</span>Data leakage</button></li><li data-full=\"67. Imagine an exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s67\" title=\"67. Imagine an exam\"><span class=\"toc-no\">67</span>Leakage: exam analogy</button></li><li data-full=\"68. Training and test data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s68\" title=\"68. Training and test data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">68</span>Train vs test data</button></li><li data-full=\"69. Where preprocessing leakage happens\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s69\" title=\"69. Where preprocessing leakage happens\"><span class=\"toc-no\">69</span>Where leakage happens</button></li><li data-full=\"70. The correct mental model\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s70\" title=\"70. The correct mental model\"><span class=\"toc-no\">70</span>Fit on train, apply to test</button></li><li data-full=\"71. One complete example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s71\" title=\"71. One complete example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">71</span>Worked example: premium purchase</button></li><li data-full=\"72. Step 1 — Business Understanding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s72\" title=\"72. Step 1 — Business Understanding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">72</span>Step 1: business goal</button></li><li data-full=\"73. Step 2 — Understand the data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s73\" title=\"73. Step 2 — Understand the data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">73</span>Step 2: inspect data</button></li><li data-full=\"74. Step 3 — Fix types\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s74\" title=\"74. Step 3 — Fix types\"><span class=\"toc-no\">74</span>Step 3: fix types</button></li><li data-full=\"75. Step 4 — Handle missing income\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s75\" title=\"75. Step 4 — Handle missing income\"><span class=\"toc-no\">75</span>Step 4: missing income</button></li><li data-full=\"76. Step 5 — Check duplicates\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s76\" title=\"76. Step 5 — Check duplicates\"><span class=\"toc-no\">76</span>Step 5: duplicates</button></li><li data-full=\"77. Step 6 — Encode categories\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s77\" title=\"77. Step 6 — Encode categories\"><span class=\"toc-no\">77</span>Step 6: encode categories</button></li><li data-full=\"78. Step 7 — Create useful date features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s78\" title=\"78. Step 7 — Create useful date features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">78</span>Step 7: date features</button></li><li data-full=\"79. Step 8 — Scale relevant numeric features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s79\" title=\"79. Step 8 — Scale relevant numeric features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">79</span>Step 8: scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"80. Step 9 — Train\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s80\" title=\"80. Step 9 — Train\"><span class=\"toc-no\">80</span>Step 9: train</button></li><li data-full=\"81. Step 10 — Evaluate\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s81\" title=\"81. Step 10 — Evaluate\"><span class=\"toc-no\">81</span>Step 10: evaluate</button></li><li data-full=\"82. Step 11 — Deployment\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s82\" title=\"82. Step 11 — Deployment\"><span class=\"toc-no\">82</span>Step 11: deploy</button></li><li data-full=\"83. The chapter is really teaching you three layers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s83\" title=\"83. The chapter is really teaching you three layers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">83</span>Three layers of the chapter</button></li><li data-full=\"84. The codes actually worth remembering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s84\" title=\"84. The codes actually worth remembering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">84</span>Key pandas code</button></li><li data-full=\"85. A scenario-answering strategy for this chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s85\" title=\"85. A scenario-answering strategy for this chapter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">85</span>Scenario answer strategy</button></li><li data-full=\"86. Example exam scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s86\" title=\"86. Example exam scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">86</span>Scenario: telecom churn</button></li><li data-full=\"87. The deepest idea in this chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2n-s87\" title=\"87. The deepest idea in this chapter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">87</span>Chapter summary</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 2 — complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with code, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>88 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Chapter 2 — Machine Learning Pipeline, taught intuitively</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Chapter 1 taught you <strong>what machine learning is</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chapter 2 is basically asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Fine. We know what ML is. How do we actually take ugly real-world data and turn it into something a model can learn from?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>And the answer is: <strong>you do not jump straight to the algorithm.</strong></p>\n<p>That is literally the first lesson of the chapter. In the equipment-failure example, the slides explicitly ask whether we start by choosing the ML algorithm and answer <strong>no</strong>—the algorithm is only one part of the workflow.</p>\n<p>The chapter is therefore mostly about this journey:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Problem → Raw Data → Clean Data → Useful Features → Model → Evaluation → Deployment\"><span>Problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Raw Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clean Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Useful Features</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deployment</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. First understand the difference: CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These two are related, but <strong>they are not the same thing</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is probably the first conceptual trap in the chapter.</p>\n<h4>CRISP-DM asks:</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do we manage the entire data science project?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Business Understanding</li>\n<li>Data Understanding</li>\n<li>Data Preparation</li>\n<li>Modeling</li>\n<li>Evaluation</li>\n<li>Deployment</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So CRISP-DM starts even before technical work.</p>\n<p>Suppose a factory manager says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Our machines keep breaking unexpectedly.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is still a <strong>business problem</strong>, not yet an ML problem.</p>\n<p>You might translate it into:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Predict whether an equipment failure will occur within the next 24 hours.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture uses almost exactly this example:</p>\n<p><strong>Business objective</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Reduce unexpected downtime.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>ML formulation</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Predict equipment failure within 24 hours.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That translation is very important.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Business problems are not automatically ML problems</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine a company says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“We want AI to improve customer satisfaction.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That tells you basically nothing.</p>\n<p>What exactly are we predicting?</p>\n<p>Maybe:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{customer churns in next 30 days})$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{expected customer satisfaction score}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{customer segment}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{recommended next product}$$</div>\n<p>The first job is therefore not:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n</code></pre>\n<p>That would be putting the cart several kilometres before the horse.</p>\n<p>The first job is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Define what success actually means.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>CRISP-DM handles this broader project thinking.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. The ML Pipeline is narrower</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_crisp_vs_pipeline\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Not two competing processes: the ML pipeline runs inside CRISP-DM&#x27;s preparation, modelling and evaluation phases.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture contrasts the two directly.</p>\n<p><strong>CRISP-DM</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Project framework</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>ML Pipeline</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Technical workflow</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>CRISP-DM deals with broader business objectives and project strategy, while the ML pipeline focuses on code, transformations and algorithms.</p>\n<p>Think of building a house.</p>\n<p>CRISP-DM is like:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why are we building it?<br />\nWho will live there?<br />\nWhat's the budget?<br />\nWhat should it achieve?<br />\nDoes the final building satisfy the client?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The ML pipeline is like:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Cement → walls → wiring → plumbing → roof.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The pipeline is the <strong>technical machinery inside the larger project</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. The standard ML pipeline</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture gives the core flow as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data Retrieval}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data Preparation}$$</div>\n<p>which contains things such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>preprocessing and wrangling</li>\n<li>feature extraction/engineering</li>\n<li>feature scaling</li>\n<li>feature selection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Modeling → Evaluation &amp; Tuning → Deployment &amp; Monitoring\"><span>Modeling</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluation &amp; Tuning</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deployment &amp; Monitoring</span></div>\n<p>The important point is that the model is <strong>late in the pipeline</strong>.</p>\n<p>A lot has to happen before training.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Why a pipeline matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine you have sensor data like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">timestamp         temperature   vibration   machine_type\n2026-01-01 08:00      75.2          3.2          A\n2026-01-01 09:00      NaN           3.6          A\n01/01/2026 10:00      81.1          ERROR        B\n</code></pre>\n<p>A model cannot magically understand:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>inconsistent date formats</li>\n<li><code>NaN</code></li>\n<li><code>\"ERROR\"</code></li>\n<li>categorical text such as <code>\"A\"</code> and <code>\"B\"</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>You need a repeatable process that turns this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">messy reality\n</code></pre>\n<p>into:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">consistent numerical features\n</code></pre>\n<p>before the model sees it.</p>\n<p>That is the pipeline.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. The single most important rule in the chapter: preprocessing consistency</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_consistency\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Parameters are learned once from training data and reused unchanged for every new record.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is one of the most important ideas here, and it's easy to underestimate.</p>\n<p>The lecture states:</p>\n<h5>During training</h5>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"TrainingData → Preprocessing → Model\"><span>TrainingData</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Preprocessing</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model</span></div>\n<h5>During deployment</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{NewData}\n\\rightarrow\n\\textbf{SAME Preprocessing}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Prediction}$$</div>\n<p>The word <strong>same</strong> is doing a lot of work.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Why must preprocessing be identical?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose training income values range from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}2,000 \\rightarrow \\text{RM}20,000$$</div>\n<p>You perform Min-Max scaling:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x' = \\frac{x-\\min}{\\max-\\min}$$</div>\n<p>So for RM10,000:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'\n=\n\\frac{10000-2000}{20000-2000}\n\\approx0.444$$</div>\n<p>The model learns using that representation.</p>\n<p>Now a new customer arrives with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10,000$$</div>\n<p>Imagine you calculate a brand-new min and max from this one person's data.</p>\n<p>That's nonsense.</p>\n<p>You might turn RM10,000 into something completely different from the representation the model was trained on.</p>\n<p>So during deployment:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Use the preprocessing parameters learned from training data.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Do not recalculate them from each new observation.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly says to learn scaling parameters and categorical mappings during training, then apply those same parameters later.</p>\n<p>This idea comes back later as <strong>data leakage</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Supervised vs unsupervised pipelines</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The preparation steps can look quite similar.</p>\n<p>The major difference is what the model learns from.</p>\n<h4>Supervised</h4>\n<p>Training data contains:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X + y$$</div>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">temperature   vibration   pressure → failure\n82.1          4.7         150      → Yes\n65.2          1.8         120      → No\n</code></pre>\n<p>The model receives both features and known labels.</p>\n<p>During prediction:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">new features → trained model → predicted label\n</code></pre>\n<p>The supervised pipeline diagram on the slides shows exactly this division between training and prediction.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Unsupervised</h4>\n<p>There is no known target $y$.</p>\n<p>You only have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X$$</div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">customer spending\nvisit frequency\nbasket size\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then the model might discover:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Cluster A\nCluster B\nCluster C\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture notes that unsupervised data still goes through the same general preparation steps before reaching the model.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>No labels does not mean no preprocessing.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Where does ML data actually come from?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter then moves backward to the start of the technical pipeline:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Data collection and retrieval.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Four major formats are emphasized:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Format</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CSV</td>\n<td>Rows and columns</td>\n<td>ML datasets, Excel exports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON</td>\n<td>Nested key-value pairs</td>\n<td>APIs, NoSQL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>XML</td>\n<td>Tag-based hierarchy</td>\n<td>Legacy enterprise systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL</td>\n<td>Relational tables</td>\n<td>Enterprise databases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The core idea isn't to memorize file extensions.</p>\n<p>Understand what shape the data arrives in.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. CSV — already close to ML's favourite shape</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>CSV naturally looks like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">name,age,score\nAli,20,85\nSara,21,91\n</code></pre>\n<p>That's basically a table:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>name</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">age</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">score</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ali</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">85</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sara</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">21</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">91</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is convenient because most classical ML expects something resembling:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Rows} = \\text{observations}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Columns} = \\text{features}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture describes CSV as one of the most widely available tabular formats.</p>\n<p>In pandas:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(&quot;data.csv&quot;)\n</code></pre>\n<p>That's enough code to understand the basic idea.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. JSON — hierarchical rather than flat</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_json_flatten\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>JSON arrives as a tree; ML wants rows and columns, so nested lists become extra rows.</figcaption></figure></div><p>JSON might look like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ali&quot;,\n  &quot;age&quot;: 20,\n  &quot;courses&quot;: [&quot;ML&quot;, &quot;Finance&quot;]\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It contains <strong>key-value pairs</strong> and can be nested. The lecture describes JSON as a lightweight data interchange format based on collections of name/value pairs.</p>\n<p>This flexibility is great for APIs.</p>\n<p>But ML often wants a flat table.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">JSON\n↓\nflatten / transform\n↓\nrows and columns\n</code></pre>\n<p>The chapter summarizes the contrast nicely:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>CSV is naturally tabular; JSON is hierarchical and may need flattening for ML.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. XML</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>XML represents information using tags:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-xml\">&lt;student&gt;\n    &lt;name&gt;Ali&lt;/name&gt;\n    &lt;age&gt;20&lt;/age&gt;\n&lt;/student&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explains its major concepts as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>tags</li>\n<li>content</li>\n<li>elements</li>\n<li>attributes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Conceptually, XML and JSON can both hold structured hierarchical information.</p>\n<p>You usually need to extract what matters and convert it into a form usable for analysis.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. HTML and web scraping</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>HTML describes webpages.</p>\n<p>But sometimes the data you want isn't sitting conveniently inside:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dataset.csv\n</code></pre>\n<p>It's displayed on a website.</p>\n<p>Then you may use <strong>web scraping</strong>.</p>\n<p>The chapter describes scraping as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Crawl</strong> — retrieve the webpage.</li>\n<li><strong>Scrape</strong> — extract the information you actually want.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Website\n↓\nHTML\n↓\nFind useful elements\n↓\nExtract values\n↓\nTabular dataset\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. SQL — where a lot of real business data lives</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Large organizations often don't have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">final_clean_dataset.csv\n</code></pre>\n<p>sitting politely on someone's desktop.</p>\n<p>Data lives in databases.</p>\n<p>You might request:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT customer_id, age, income, churn\nFROM customers;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture discusses direct SQL access through tools such as SQLAlchemy and pyodbc, as well as object-relational mapping.</p>\n<p>For this chapter, the conceptual point is simply:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Database}\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{query}}\n\\text{Required Data}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Before cleaning anything: understand data types</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter then makes another crucial point:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Different kinds of data require different preprocessing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It gives these broad categories:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Typical preprocessing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Numeric</td>\n<td>Age, Price</td>\n<td>Scaling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Categorical</td>\n<td>Red, Medium</td>\n<td>Encoding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>Review sentence</td>\n<td>Tokenization etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Datetime</td>\n<td>Timestamp</td>\n<td>Extract month/day/duration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>You cannot treat all columns the same.</p>\n<p>That's why blindly applying transformations to every column is a terrible idea.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Numeric data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Examples:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 25\nPrice = 99.99\nIncome = 60000\n</code></pre>\n<p>Algorithms already understand numbers.</p>\n<p>But the <strong>scale</strong> can matter.</p>\n<p>We'll come back to that.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Categorical data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Examples:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Colour = Red\nPlan = Premium\nMachine_Type = B\n</code></pre>\n<p>These aren't quantities.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Premium\n</code></pre>\n<p>is not naturally:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2.7$$</div>\n<p>We need to encode categories numerically without accidentally inventing relationships that aren't real.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Datetime data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-10-03 15:30\n</code></pre>\n<p>A datetime can contain many possible features:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">year\nmonth\nday\nhour\nweekday\nweekend?\n</code></pre>\n<p>So often the raw timestamp itself isn't the useful information.</p>\n<p>You derive useful information from it.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.month\ndf[&quot;is_weekend&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.dayofweek &gt; 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is one of the feature-engineering examples used in the lecture.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Text data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A sentence:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;The product was absolutely terrible.&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>isn't directly a clean numerical feature.</p>\n<p>You may need things such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">tokenization\nstopword removal\nstemming\nlemmatization\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture covers these later. We'll return to them.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Data wrangling: the dirty work that actually matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture defines data wrangling as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>cleaning, transforming, restructuring and preparing raw data for analysis and machine learning.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Messy Raw Data → Wrangling → ML-Ready Data\"><span>Messy Raw Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Wrangling</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>ML-Ready Data</span></div>\n<p>And the chapter repeats the old rule:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Garbage In} \\rightarrow \\text{Garbage Out}}$$</div>\n<p>A sophisticated algorithm cannot rescue nonsensical data.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Example: what's wrong with this dataset?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter uses transaction data like:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Product ID</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Quantity</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Price</th>\n<th>User Type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2023-01-15</td>\n<td>P-101</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">150</td>\n<td>New</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2023-01-15</td>\n<td>P-105</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">NaN</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">85.5</td>\n<td>Returning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16/01/2023</td>\n<td>P-101</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">150</td>\n<td>Premium</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Immediately notice:</p>\n<h5>Problem 1 — inconsistent dates</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2023-01-15\n</code></pre>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">16/01/2023\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Problem 2 — missing values</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">NaN\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Problem 3 — potentially inconvenient column naming</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Quantity Purchased\n</code></pre>\n<p>contains spaces.</p>\n<p>The lecture deliberately uses these issues as the starting point for wrangling.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. First rule of cleaning: inspect before touching</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You don't immediately start deleting rows.</p>\n<p>First:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Understand what you've actually got.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Two very important pandas tools in this chapter are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.info()\ndf.describe()\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>info()</code> helps reveal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>row count</li>\n<li>columns</li>\n<li>data types</li>\n<li>non-null counts</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>describe()</code> gives statistical summaries such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>mean</li>\n<li>standard deviation</li>\n<li>minimum</li>\n<li>maximum</li>\n</ul>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Why <code>info()</code> is useful</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Price        float64\nDate         object\nQuantity     int64\nUser_Type    object\n</code></pre>\n<p>You look at:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Date → object\n</code></pre>\n<p>and think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Wait. Why isn't that datetime?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That immediately tells you there may be a type problem.</p>\n<p>Likewise:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">1000 rows\nPrice → 930 non-null\n</code></pre>\n<p>means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$70\\ \\text{values missing}$$</div>\n<p>So <code>info()</code> is like checking the patient before prescribing medicine.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Why <code>describe()</code> is useful</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age\nmean = 31\nmin = 18\nmax = 953\n</code></pre>\n<p>You probably found something suspicious.</p>\n<p>Nobody should blindly conclude:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Ah yes, our average 31-year-old customer has one 953-year-old colleague.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Either:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>data-entry error</li>\n<li>coding problem</li>\n<li>unit problem</li>\n<li>outlier</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>describe()</code> helps expose these quickly.</p>\n<p>The chapter specifically notes that statistical summaries are useful for identifying extreme values.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. Data quality checklist</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter highlights four major things to inspect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Missing Values}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Wrong Data Types}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Duplicates}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Extreme Outliers}\n}$$</div>\n<p>because each can silently harm the model.</p>\n<p>That checklist is worth remembering.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Clean column names</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;Quantity Purchased&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coding repeatedly becomes annoying:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;Quantity Purchased&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<p>You may rename it:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.rename(\n    columns={&quot;Quantity Purchased&quot;: &quot;quantity&quot;},\n    inplace=True\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture uses renaming as part of making attributes more readable and consistent.</p>\n<p>This sounds trivial.</p>\n<p>It is trivial.</p>\n<p>It still saves unnecessary pain later.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Selecting columns</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose your DataFrame has:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">date\nprice\nproduct_id\nquantity\nserial_no\nuser_id\nuser_type\n</code></pre>\n<p>but you only want:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">price\n</code></pre>\n<p>By name:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;price&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or by location:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.iloc[:, 1]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture also introduces selecting based on data type.</p>\n<p>The intuition:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Column selection asks: which variables do I want?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Filtering rows</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now instead suppose you want:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Only transactions above RM100.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">high_value = df[df[&quot;price&quot;] &gt; 100]\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is <strong>row filtering</strong>.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Column selection → which variables?\nRow filtering → which observations?\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture shows filtering by indices, removing specified rows, logical conditions, and top/bottom offsets.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Data types can look correct to humans but be wrong to computers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;2023-01-15&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>You and I know that's a date.</p>\n<p>Python may only know:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">string\n</code></pre>\n<p>If it remains a string, something like:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>days since purchase</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>becomes awkward or impossible.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;date&quot;] = pd.to_datetime(df[&quot;date&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly introduces <code>to_datetime()</code> for this purpose.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Numeric strings</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Similarly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;$150.00&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>looks numeric to us.</p>\n<p>But the dollar sign can make it text.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;$150.00&quot;\n↓\nremove symbol\n↓\n150.00\n↓\nnumeric\n</code></pre>\n<p>The chapter also introduces <code>pd.to_numeric()</code> for converting numeric-looking text into real numeric values.</p>\n<p>The bigger lesson:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The value and the data type are separate things.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A number stored as text isn't automatically usable as a number.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Feature engineering — turning existing information into better information</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is one of the most important ML ideas in the whole course.</p>\n<p>Feature engineering means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use existing information to produce a representation that makes the useful pattern easier for the model to learn.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The chapter describes it as deriving new useful values from existing information.</p>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Date = 2026-10-03\n</code></pre>\n<p>Maybe the exact date itself is not useful.</p>\n<p>But:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Month = 10\nWeekend = True\n</code></pre>\n<p>might be useful.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Example: derive weekend</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;is_weekend&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.dayofweek &gt; 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Suppose restaurant sales are consistently higher on weekends.</p>\n<p>The raw date:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-10-03\n</code></pre>\n<p>doesn't explicitly tell the model:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Saturday.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The engineered feature does.</p>\n<p>This is exactly the kind of thinking feature engineering is about.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. <code>map()</code> — convert values using a mapping</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">New\nReturning\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can create:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">mapping = {\n    &quot;New&quot;: 1,\n    &quot;Returning&quot;: 2\n}\n\ndf[&quot;user_code&quot;] = df[&quot;user_type&quot;].map(mapping)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture uses mapping to transform categorical values and also discusses <code>apply()</code>, <code>applymap()</code> and <code>map()</code> as transformation utilities.</p>\n<p>The intuition behind <code>map()</code> is simply:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">old value → corresponding new value\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Missing values</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we reach one of the biggest practical problems.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Product</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">120</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">NaN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">110</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>What should C be?</p>\n<p>There is no universally correct answer.</p>\n<p>And that's exactly why missing-data handling is important.</p>\n<p>The lecture says missing values can cause algorithm errors, distort statistics and lead to major data loss if rows are blindly discarded.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. Strategy 1: Remove</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Simplest:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.dropna()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Meaning:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If information is missing, throw away that observation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This may be reasonable when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>dataset is large</li>\n<li>very few values are missing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture gives a rough example of very low missingness, such as below 1%.</p>\n<p>But notice the danger.</p>\n<p>Suppose you have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000\\ \\text{rows}$$</div>\n<p>and 2 rows have missing values.</p>\n<p>Fine.</p>\n<p>Suppose you have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000\\ \\text{rows}$$</div>\n<p>and 450 contain missing values.</p>\n<p><code>dropna()</code> could leave you with half a dataset.</p>\n<p>Brutal simplicity isn't always wisdom.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Strategy 2: Impute</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of removing the row:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Replace the missing value.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For numeric values:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;price&quot;] = df[&quot;price&quot;].fillna(df[&quot;price&quot;].mean())\n</code></pre>\n<p>The chapter describes mean and median as common central-tendency choices.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Mean vs median</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_impute\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Mean versus median imputation</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Change the outlier and see why the median is the safer fill value.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Return to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">100\n120\nNaN\n110\n1000\n</code></pre>\n<p>Mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{100+120+110+1000}{4}\n=\n332.5$$</div>\n<p>Does 332.5 look representative of most values?</p>\n<p>Not really.</p>\n<p>The value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000$$</div>\n<p>pulls the mean upward.</p>\n<p>Sorted data:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">100, 110, 120, 1000\n</code></pre>\n<p>Median:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{110+120}{2}\n=\n115$$</div>\n<p>That is much closer to the typical observations.</p>\n<p>So the lecture recommends:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>mean</strong> for numeric data without problematic outliers</li>\n<li><strong>median</strong> when outliers matter</li>\n</ul>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Strategy 3: Forward fill / backward fill</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose temperature readings are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">10:00 → 25°C\n10:01 → NaN\n10:02 → 25.2°C\n</code></pre>\n<p>You might assume the previous condition persists temporarily.</p>\n<p>Forward fill:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.ffill()\n</code></pre>\n<p>turns:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">25\nNaN\n25.2\n</code></pre>\n<p>into:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">25\n25\n25.2\n</code></pre>\n<p>Backward fill does the reverse:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">next known value\n↑\nfills previous missing value\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture particularly associates forward/backward filling with ordered or time-related data where nearby observations may reasonably carry information.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. Important: missing-value handling is a reasoning problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Do not think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>NaN = mean.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's not a rule.</p>\n<p>You need to ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why is it missing?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose <code>salary</code> is missing because high-income people deliberately refuse to report it.</p>\n<p>Then filling all missing salaries with the average could introduce bias.</p>\n<p>The chapter doesn't go deeply into missing-data mechanisms, but its practical message is already enough:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Choose the strategy based on the data and context, not habit.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Duplicates</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Transaction 401:\nCoffee, RM8.50, 9:00 AM\n\nTransaction 402:\nCoffee, RM8.50, 9:00 AM\n</code></pre>\n<p>Are those duplicates?</p>\n<p>Maybe.</p>\n<p>Or perhaps someone genuinely bought two coffees.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically warns:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Do not automatically delete duplicate-looking records without understanding whether they represent legitimate repeated events.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is a very good rule.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. Why duplicates are dangerous</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose one transaction accidentally appears 50 times.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Mean}$$</div>\n<p>can get distorted.</p>\n<p>Counts become wrong.</p>\n<p>And worse:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The same duplicate might appear in both training and test datasets.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Then the model sees the answer during training and later appears wonderfully intelligent on the test set.</p>\n<p>It isn't intelligent.</p>\n<p>You accidentally gave it the exam paper.</p>\n<p>The chapter explicitly mentions this as a potential form of data leakage.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. Basic duplicate handling</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Check:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.duplicated().sum()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Remove exact duplicates:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.drop_duplicates(inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Again:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Check first.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Do not reflexively delete.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. Categorical data — computers don't naturally understand <code>\"Red\"</code></span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red\nBlue\nGreen\n</code></pre>\n<p>Most ML algorithms fundamentally operate numerically.</p>\n<p>The chapter therefore asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we translate categories into numbers without misleading the algorithm?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Two common approaches here are:</p>\n<p><strong>Label encoding</strong></p>\n<p>and</p>\n<p><strong>One-hot encoding</strong></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. Label encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red   → 0\nBlue  → 1\nGreen → 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>Very compact.</p>\n<p>But here's the problem.</p>\n<p>The model may interpret:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Green} &gt; \\text{Blue} &gt; \\text{Red}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Distance}(\\text{Green},\\text{Red})=2$$</div>\n<p>That mathematical ordering may have absolutely no real-world meaning.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly highlights this risk.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">45. When numerical coding makes sense</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose categories are genuinely ordered:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Low\nMedium\nHigh\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Low    = 0\nMedium = 1\nHigh   = 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>may make conceptual sense because there really is an ordering.</p>\n<p>But:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Malaysia = 0\nJapan    = 1\nBrazil   = 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>doesn't mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Brazil} &gt; \\text{Japan} &gt; \\text{Malaysia}$$</div>\n<p>That's nonsense.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">46. One-Hot Encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead, create one column per category.</p>\n<p>Original:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Colour = Red\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red  Blue  Green\n1     0      0\n</code></pre>\n<p>Blue:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0     1      0\n</code></pre>\n<p>Green:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0     0      1\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture describes exactly this structure and notes that one-hot encoding avoids implied order, making it suitable for nominal categories.</p>\n<p>With pandas:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[&quot;user_type&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>That's enough code to understand the mechanism.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">47. Label encoding vs One-Hot Encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Use this intuition:</p>\n<h5>Label/ordinal representation</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>There is meaningful order.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Poor &lt; Average &lt; Good &lt; Excellent\n</code></pre>\n<h5>One-hot representation</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Different categories, but no meaningful ordering.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red\nBlue\nGreen\n</code></pre>\n<p>The chapter demonstrates both direct mapping and <code>get_dummies()</code> for categorical variables.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s48\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">48. Feature scaling — why numbers can unfairly dominate</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_scaling\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Why feature scaling matters</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · How much of the distance between two customers comes from age versus income, before and after scaling.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose two features are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 25\nIncome = 45000\n</code></pre>\n<p>and another person:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 30\nIncome = 55000\n</code></pre>\n<p>Difference in age:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$30-25=5$$</div>\n<p>Difference in income:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$55000-45000=10000$$</div>\n<p>If an algorithm uses distances, income numerically dwarfs age.</p>\n<p>Not necessarily because income is <strong>more important</strong>, but simply because the units are larger.</p>\n<p>The lecture gives exactly this kind of Age-vs-Income example and notes that distance-based algorithms such as KNN or SVM can be affected.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">49. Scaling fixes the measuring-stick problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine measuring:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age in years\nIncome in RM\nDistance in meters\nWeight in kg\n</code></pre>\n<p>The scales are arbitrary.</p>\n<p>Changing income from:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">RM45,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">45 thousand RM\n</code></pre>\n<p>shouldn't fundamentally change how important income is.</p>\n<p>But without scaling, it might.</p>\n<p>Feature scaling puts variables onto more comparable numerical footing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">50. Min-Max Scaling</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes Min-Max scaling as compressing values between:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0 \\text{ and } 1$$</div>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'\n=\n\\frac{x-x_{\\text{min}}}\n{x_{\\text{max}}-x_{\\text{min}}}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose ages are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">20\n30\n40\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p>For 20:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{20-20}{40-20}=0$$</div>\n<p>For 30:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{30-20}{40-20}=0.5$$</div>\n<p>For 40:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div>\n<p>So:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">20 → 0\n30 → 0.5\n40 → 1\n</code></pre>\n<p>Same ordering.</p>\n<p>Different scale.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s51\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">51. Standardization / Z-score</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter's second major scaling method is standardization:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>center data around mean 0 with standard deviation 1.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z = \\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\mu = \\text{mean}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma = \\text{standard deviation}$$</div>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=0$$</div>\n<p>the observation is at the mean.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=+2$$</div>\n<p>it's two standard deviations above the mean.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=-1$$</div>\n<p>it's one standard deviation below.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s52\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">52. Scaling does NOT mean deleting information</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">RM20,000\nRM40,000\nRM80,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes approximately:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0\n0.33\n1\n</code></pre>\n<p>You haven't destroyed the ordering.</p>\n<p>You've transformed the representation.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Same pattern, different ruler.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s53\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">53. Does every algorithm require scaling?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture emphasizes scaling where feature magnitudes can affect calculations, especially distance-based methods.</p>\n<p>So don't memorize:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>ML = always scale everything.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The better lesson is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Understand whether the algorithm is sensitive to feature magnitude.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s54\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">54. Text preprocessing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;The cats are running!&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Raw text contains lots of stuff that may not help.</p>\n<p>The chapter gives a typical flow:</p>\n<h5>Tokenization</h5>\n<p>Break text into units:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[&quot;The&quot;, &quot;cats&quot;, &quot;are&quot;, &quot;running&quot;, &quot;!&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Stopword removal</h5>\n<p>Remove frequent low-information words such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">the\nis\nat\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Stemming / Lemmatization</h5>\n<p>Normalize word forms:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">running → run\ncats → cat\n</code></pre>\n<p>Giving something like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[&quot;cat&quot;, &quot;run&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s55\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">55. Why text preprocessing exists</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Consider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">run\nruns\nrunning\nran\n</code></pre>\n<p>To a naïve model these may appear to be four completely unrelated tokens.</p>\n<p>But semantically they are strongly related.</p>\n<p>Text preprocessing tries to reduce some of that mess.</p>\n<p>The lecture distinguishes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>stemming as a heuristic reduction toward a root</li>\n<li>lemmatization as using linguistic rules and vocabulary</li>\n</ul>\n<p>and describes stopword removal as reducing high-frequency low-information words.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s56\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">56. Data summarization: stop staring at 1,000 rows</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1,000,000\\ \\text{transactions}$$</div>\n<p>Nobody wants to manually inspect:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">row 1\nrow 2\nrow 3\n...\nrow 1,000,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>You summarize.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Average price by user type.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture describes summarization as creating compact representations through aggregation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s57\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">57. Understand <code>groupby()</code> using Split → Apply → Combine</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_groupby\"><div class=\"vis-title\">groupby step by step</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Step through split → apply → combine.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is the best way to understand it.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>User</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">700</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>We ask:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Step 1 — Split</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">New:\n100\n200\n\nPremium:\n500\n700\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Step 2 — Apply</h5>\n<p>Mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{New} = 150$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Premium} = 600$$</div>\n<h5>Step 3 — Combine</h5>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>user_type</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">mean price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">600</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture explicitly calls this:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Split → Apply → Combine\"><span>Split</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Apply</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Combine</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s58\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">58. Multiple aggregations</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Sometimes one statistic isn't enough.</p>\n<p>Maybe you want:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">mean price\nmaximum price\ntotal quantity\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;).agg({\n    &quot;price&quot;: [&quot;mean&quot;, &quot;max&quot;],\n    &quot;quantity&quot;: &quot;sum&quot;\n})\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture shows increasingly complex forms of grouping and aggregation across multiple attributes.</p>\n<p>Again, don't memorize syntax first.</p>\n<p>Understand the question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Group by what, then calculate what?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s59\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">59. Visualization — don't model what you haven't looked at</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter gives a good rule:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Before training a model, understand your data.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Visualization helps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify trends</li>\n<li>compare groups</li>\n<li>spot outliers</li>\n<li>discover relationships</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This isn't just for making slides look pretty.</p>\n<p>Visualization is diagnostic.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s60\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">60. Chart selection should follow the question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_chart_picker\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Choosing a chart</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Pick the question; the right chart follows.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives this mapping:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Chart</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trend over time?</td>\n<td>Line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare categories?</td>\n<td>Bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution of one numeric variable?</td>\n<td>Histogram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outliers and spread?</td>\n<td>Box plot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationship between two numeric variables?</td>\n<td>Scatter plot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is worth understanding rather than memorizing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s61\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">61. Line chart = how something changes</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Month vs sales\nHour vs electricity demand\nDate vs stock price\n</code></pre>\n<p>Question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What happened over time?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Use a line.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s62\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">62. Bar chart = compare categories</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Sales by branch\n</code></pre>\n<p>You want:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Ipoh       ███████\nKL         ███████████\nPenang     █████\n</code></pre>\n<p>The categories are discrete.</p>\n<p>You're comparing quantities across them.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s63\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">63. Histogram = what does one numerical variable look like?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose prices are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">10, 11, 12, 13, 15, 18, 100, 120...\n</code></pre>\n<p>A histogram asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Where are values concentrated?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It groups observations into numerical ranges.</p>\n<p>Useful for seeing:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">skewness\nclusters\nlong tails\nunusual values\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture's the slides uses a histogram to show the distribution of product prices.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s64\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">64. Box plot = spread + unusual values</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A box plot helps answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Where is the centre?<br />\nHow spread out is the data?<br />\nAre there extreme observations?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is especially useful for quickly spotting potential outliers.</p>\n<p>The chapter pairs box plots with identifying spread and outliers.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s65\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">65. Scatter plot = relationship between two numeric variables</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X = \\text{advertising spend}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Y = \\text{sales}$$</div>\n<p>Each point represents one observation.</p>\n<p>If points generally rise:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">        •\n      •\n    •\n  •\n•\n</code></pre>\n<p>you may have a positive association.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies scatter plots as the appropriate basic choice for relationships between two numerical variables.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s66\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">66. Now the concept that ruins a shocking number of ML experiments: data leakage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The final assessment asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What is Data Leakage in preprocessing?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The chapter answers:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Using information from the test set, such as recalculating scaling parameters from it, which falsely inflates accuracy.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This deserves a proper explanation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s67\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">67. Imagine an exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">80 questions for studying\n20 questions for final exam\n</code></pre>\n<p>You promise:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I'll use the 20 exam questions only to test myself.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Then before studying, you calculate:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Which topics appear most frequently in the exam?\nWhich answers appear?\nWhat vocabulary is used?\n</code></pre>\n<p>And use that information while preparing.</p>\n<p>Then you score 95%.</p>\n<p>Was that a fair test?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>Information from the final exam influenced your preparation.</p>\n<p>That is basically what data leakage does.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s68\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">68. Training and test data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Conceptually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Dataset\n├── Training\n└── Test\n</code></pre>\n<p>Training:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Learn.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Test:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Pretend these observations are future unseen cases.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The test set should simulate data the model hasn't had access to.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s69\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">69. Where preprocessing leakage happens</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_leakage\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Data leakage in scaling</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Fit the scaler on training data only, or on everything — and see what leaks.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Train} = [10,20,30]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Test} = [100]$$</div>\n<p>If you calculate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{max}=100$$</div>\n<p>using the entire dataset, then information from test data has already affected preprocessing.</p>\n<p>Instead:</p>\n<h5>Training</h5>\n<p>Learn:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{min}=10,\\quad \\text{max}=30$$</div>\n<p>Then apply <strong>those exact values</strong> when transforming the test observation.</p>\n<p>This is precisely why the chapter earlier emphasized preprocessing consistency.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s70\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">70. The correct mental model</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>During training:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">TRAIN DATA\n   ↓\nlearn preprocessing rules\n   ↓\ntransform train data\n   ↓\ntrain model\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">TEST / FUTURE DATA\n   ↓\napply already-learned preprocessing rules\n   ↓\nmodel prediction\n</code></pre>\n<p>Not:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">TEST DATA\n   ↓\nlearn new preprocessing rules\n</code></pre>\n<p>That would allow future information to creep backward into training.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s71\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">71. One complete example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose our goal is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Predict whether a customer will purchase a premium subscription.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Raw dataset:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ID</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Age</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Income</th>\n<th>Plan</th>\n<th>Signup</th>\n<th>Purchased</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">45</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">60000</td>\n<td>Basic</td>\n<td>01-Jan-25</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">22</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">NaN</td>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td>15-Feb-25</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">45</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">60000</td>\n<td>Basic</td>\n<td>01-Jan-25</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Now reason through the pipeline.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s72\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">72. Step 1 — Business Understanding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Business asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which customers are likely to purchase premium?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Turn into ML:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X \\rightarrow y$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$y =\n\\{\\text{Yes},\\text{No}\\}$$</div>\n<p>This is supervised classification.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s73\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">73. Step 2 — Understand the data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Check:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.info()\ndf.describe()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Immediately notice:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Income → missing\n</code></pre>\n<p>Potential duplicate:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">row 1 == row 3\n</code></pre>\n<p>Categorical:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Plan\n</code></pre>\n<p>Date:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Signup\n</code></pre>\n<p>possibly stored as string.</p>\n<p>This mirrors the chapter's pipeline activity on page 104.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s74\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">74. Step 3 — Fix types</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;signup&quot;] = pd.to_datetime(df[&quot;signup&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now date operations are possible.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s75\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">75. Step 4 — Handle missing income</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Perhaps use median:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;income&quot;] = df[&quot;income&quot;].fillna(\n    df[&quot;income&quot;].median()\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>But only after thinking about why it is missing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s76\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">76. Step 5 — Check duplicates</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.duplicated().sum()\n</code></pre>\n<p>If row 1 and 3 are accidental copies:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.drop_duplicates(inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If they represent genuine repeated events, keep them.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s77\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">77. Step 6 — Encode categories</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><code>Plan</code> might become:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Basic   Premium\n1       0\n0       1\n</code></pre>\n<p>through one-hot encoding.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s78\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">78. Step 7 — Create useful date features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Maybe:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;signup_month&quot;] = df[&quot;signup&quot;].dt.month\n</code></pre>\n<p>Could month affect purchasing behaviour?</p>\n<p>Maybe.</p>\n<p>That's feature engineering.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s79\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">79. Step 8 — Scale relevant numeric features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age:       18 → 70\nIncome: 20000 → 500000\n</code></pre>\n<p>If using a scale-sensitive algorithm, put them onto comparable footing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s80\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">80. Step 9 — Train</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Only now do we reach:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Notice how late that came.</p>\n<p>That is one of the chapter's core lessons.</p>\n<p>ML is not:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">load file\n↓\nmodel.fit()\n↓\ndone\n</code></pre>\n<p>Reality is considerably less considerate.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s81\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">81. Step 10 — Evaluate</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Use genuinely unseen data.</p>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does this actually satisfy the business goal?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's also where CRISP-DM reconnects with the technical pipeline.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly says evaluation should ask whether the model meets the business criteria defined earlier; if not, iterate.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s82\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">82. Step 11 — Deployment</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now future customers arrive:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">new customer\n↓\nsame cleaning\n↓\nsame categorical mapping\n↓\nsame scaling parameters\n↓\ntrained model\n↓\nprediction\n</code></pre>\n<p>Not:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">new customer\n↓\ninvent entirely new preprocessing\n↓\nhope\n</code></pre>\n<p>Consistency matters.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s83\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">83. The chapter is really teaching you three layers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of remembering 109 slides individually, organize them like this.</p>\n<h4>Layer 1 — Project thinking</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CRISP}\\text{-}\\text{DM}$$</div>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">What problem?\nWhat data?\nWhat success criterion?\nHow will this be deployed?\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h4>Layer 2 — Data preparation</h4>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">What format?\nWhat types?\nMissing?\nDuplicates?\nIncorrect types?\nCategories?\nNeed scaling?\nCan we engineer better features?\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is the bulk of the chapter.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Layer 3 — ML workflow</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Prepare\n↓\nTrain\n↓\nEvaluate\n↓\nDeploy\n↓\nMonitor\n</code></pre>\n<p>And iterate when needed.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s84\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">84. The codes actually worth remembering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You don't need fifty almost-identical snippets.</p>\n<p>These capture most of the chapter's practical ideas:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Inspect\ndf.info()\ndf.describe()\n\n# Missing values\ndf.isna().sum()\ndf[&quot;price&quot;].fillna(df[&quot;price&quot;].median())\ndf.dropna()\n\n# Duplicates\ndf.duplicated().sum()\ndf.drop_duplicates()\n\n# Datetime\ndf[&quot;date&quot;] = pd.to_datetime(df[&quot;date&quot;])\n\n# Filter\ndf[df[&quot;price&quot;] &gt; 100]\n\n# Feature engineering\ndf[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.month\n\n# Categorical encoding\npd.get_dummies(df, columns=[&quot;user_type&quot;])\n\n# Summarization\ndf.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Everything else is mostly a variation of these concepts.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s85\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">85. A scenario-answering strategy for this chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If an exam gives you a dataset or business scenario, think in this order:</p>\n<p><strong>First:</strong> What business problem are we solving?</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Business Problem}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{ML Task}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Second:</strong> What data do we have?</p>\n<p>Look for:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">numeric\ncategorical\ntext\ndatetime\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Third:</strong> What is dirty?</p>\n<p>Check:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">missing\nduplicates\nwrong types\noutliers\ninconsistency\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Fourth:</strong> What transformations are necessary?</p>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Need encoding?\nNeed scaling?\nNeed new features?\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Fifth:</strong> Can preprocessing leak information?</p>\n<p>Make sure transformations are <strong>learned from training data</strong> and reused later.</p>\n<p><strong>Sixth:</strong> What summary or visualization helps me understand the data?</p>\n<p>Ask what you actually want to know before choosing a chart.</p>\n<p><strong>Finally:</strong> Train and evaluate the model.</p>\n<p>This order matters.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s86\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">86. Example exam scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>A telecom company wants to predict customer churn. The dataset contains Age, Monthly Charges, Contract Type, Signup Date and Churn. Some Monthly Charges are missing and Contract Type contains Monthly, Annual and Two-Year categories.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Reason through it.</p>\n<h5>Business goal</h5>\n<p>Reduce customer churn.</p>\n<h5>ML formulation</h5>\n<p>Predict:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Churn} = \\text{Yes/No}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Supervised Classification}$$</div>\n<h5>Missing data</h5>\n<p><code>Monthly Charges</code></p>\n<p>Investigate and impute/remove appropriately.</p>\n<h5>Categorical feature</h5>\n<p><code>Contract Type</code></p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Monthly\nAnnual\nTwo-Year\n</code></pre>\n<p>are categorical.</p>\n<p>Depending on the intended representation, encode them rather than feeding raw strings.</p>\n<h5>Datetime</h5>\n<p><code>Signup Date</code></p>\n<p>Convert to datetime.</p>\n<p>Potentially derive:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CustomerTenure}$$</div>\n<p>which may be more useful than the raw date.</p>\n<h5>Numerical features</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age\nMonthly Charges\nTenure\n</code></pre>\n<p>Scale where required by the chosen algorithm.</p>\n<h5>Critical pipeline rule</h5>\n<p>Learn preprocessing from training data and reuse it on test/future customers.</p>\n<p>That is a much stronger answer than:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Use Random Forest.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2n-s87\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">87. The deepest idea in this chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Chapter 1's big lesson was:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Machine Learning} = \\text{Learn patterns that generalize}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Chapter 2's big lesson is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nA\\ \\text{good model starts long before model}.\\text{fit}()\n}$$</div>\n<p>The slides end with essentially the same idea:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Machine learning is a workflow; raw data must be inspected and cleaned; categories need encoding, numeric ranges may need scaling, and useful features may need to be derived.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>And the final slide reduces good ML to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Good Data}\n+\n\\text{Solid Preparation}\n+\n\\text{Proper Evaluation}\n}$$</div>\n<p>So if you remember only the whole chapter as one flow, remember this:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Understand Problem → Collect Data → Inspect → Clean → Transform → Engineer Features → Train → Evaluate → Deploy\"><span>Understand Problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Collect Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Inspect</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clean</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transform</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Engineer Features</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Train</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deploy</span></div>\n<p>And throughout all of it:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Whatever preprocessing you learn during training must be applied consistently later.}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That last point—especially the connection between <strong>preprocessing consistency and data leakage</strong>—is probably the concept from this lecture I'd make sure you genuinely understand rather than merely memorize.</p></div></details></div></details>");
